Description: CVE-2025-68973 - Fix possible memory corruption in the armor parser
 The armor_filter() function in g10/armor.c contained a faulty double
 increment of the destination index "n" when copying data from
 AFX->BUFFER to BUF: the loop incremented "n" both in its increment
 expression and inside the loop body (buf[n++] = ...). This caused every
 second byte of the output buffer to be left uninitialized and could lead
 to writing past the intended range, resulting in memory corruption. The
 code path is only reachable with specially crafted input, but memory
 corruption can never be tolerated as it always carries the potential for
 remote code execution. The fix removes the extraneous increment in the
 loop's increment expression so "n" is only advanced once per copied byte.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/commit/1e929abd20fa2e4be3797a137caca63a971d5372
Author: Werner Koch <wk@gnupg.org>
Last-Update: 2026-07-29
---
--- a/g10/armor.c
+++ b/g10/armor.c
@@ -1026,8 +1026,8 @@ armor_filter( void *opaque, int control,
 	n = 0;
 	if( afx->buffer_len ) {
             /* Copy the data from AFX->BUFFER to BUF.  */
-	    for(; n < size && afx->buffer_pos < afx->buffer_len; n++ )
-		buf[n++] = afx->buffer[afx->buffer_pos++];
+            for(; n < size && afx->buffer_pos < afx->buffer_len;)
+                buf[n++] = afx->buffer[afx->buffer_pos++];
 	    if( afx->buffer_pos >= afx->buffer_len )
 		afx->buffer_len = 0;
 	}
